INT. MARLOW FARMHOUSE - KITCHEN - EARLY MORNING

A cold stove. A table with three mismatched chairs. A single teacup, unwashed, on the sill.

CONSTANCE, 40s, stands at the sink with her coat still buttoned. She does not turn on the tap. Her sister HATTIE, 30s, enters from the hall in a nightgown and bare feet, hugging her elbows. At the table, WALTER, 70s, their father, sits upright in his Sunday collar, a folded newspaper in front of him that he has not opened.

She watches the back of her sister's head. She says nothing. Hattie crosses to the stove, strikes a match, and stands holding it until the flame reaches her fingers.

CONSTANCE
You'll burn yourself.

HATTIE
I know.

She shakes it out. The smoke curls toward the ceiling.

WALTER
Sit down, both of you.

Neither sits. Constance finally turns on the tap, and the water runs over a plate she isn't washing.

HATTIE
She came back last night.

CONSTANCE
Who did?

HATTIE
You know who.

Walter unfolds the paper, folds it again, smoother this time. He sets it square on the table.

WALTER
Your mother is in the churchyard. We buried her Tuesday.

She does not look at him. She keeps her eyes on her sister's hands, shaking now, the matchbox rattling in them. Hattie slides it across the counter, away from herself, toward the sink.

CONSTANCE
(turning off the tap)
Take that outside.

HATTIE
Why?

CONSTANCE
Because Father is looking at it.

Walter is. He is looking at the matchbox as if it were an animal. Slowly he reaches out and lays his big, spotted hand over it. Neither daughter moves. Outside, a rooster crows, late.
